Now, there's a subtle issue. In swapWorld, when the profile changes, the background is rebuilt:
```ts
if (key !== this.profileKey) {
  this.scene.remove(this.background.sky, this.background.stars, this.dome.mesh);
  this.background.dispose();
  this.dome.dispose();
  ...
  this.scene.add(this.background.sky, this.background.stars, this.dome.mesh);
  ...
}
```
This removes and re-adds sky/stars/dome but NOT the sun. The sun stays in the scene. But the ordering — after this block, the sun was added before the dome originally. When the background is rebuilt, sky and stars get new objects (new higher ids than the sun now!). Wait — that's a problem. When a profile change happens, createBackground creates NEW sky and stars objects with HIGHER object ids than the existing sun. So the new stars (renderOrder 1) would have a higher id than the sun (renderOrder 1), meaning the stars would draw OVER the sun after a profile change!

Hmm. But wait — does this actually matter? The stars are tiny 1.8px points. Even if they draw over the sun, they're sparse points and won't dim the sun's 4° disk measurably. The e2e brightness assertion (8×8 > 150) would still pass because a few star points over a ~26px-wide bright disk won't drop the 8×8 center below 150.

But to be correct and match the spec ("add it to the scene AFTER the stars so it draws over them"), I should ensure the sun is added after the (re)built stars even on profile change. Let me handle this: in the profile-change block of swapWorld, after re-adding the background, re-add the sun (or remove+re-add it after the stars). 

Actually, the cleanest fix: in the profile-change branch, after `this.scene.add(this.background.sky, this.background.stars, this.dome.mesh);`, do `this.scene.remove(this.sun.mesh); this.scene.add(this.sun.mesh);` to re-append the sun so it's after the new stars in scene order (and re-add doesn't change its object id, but scene order affects... hmm, actually the id is what matters for the tie-break, not scene-add order).

Wait, let me reconsider. The tie-break in three.js for equal renderOrder is by object id (not scene insertion order). Actually, let me verify. In three.js, the transparent objects are sorted by the `PainterSortStable` which uses `groupOrder` (renderOrder), then z (depth), then id. The id is the Object3D's unique id (assigned at construction). So scene insertion order does NOT matter for the tie-break — only the id does.

So when the background is rebuilt with new stars (higher id than the sun), the new stars would sort after the sun (higher id) → stars draw over sun. That's a minor visual issue (sparse star points over the sun disk) but technically the sun no longer draws "last".

To fix this properly, I could give the sun a renderOrder higher than the stars. But the spec explicitly says renderOrder must be an integer and to use renderOrder 1 (not 1.5). Hmm. But the spec also says renderOrder 2 is the dome. So renderOrder 1 for the sun, 0 sky, 1 stars, 2 dome. The sun and stars share renderOrder 1.

Wait — actually, let me reconsider. Is the tie-break by id or by scene order? Let me check three.js more carefully. In WebGLRenderer, transparent objects are sorted with:
```js
customOpaqueSort or the default:
function painterSortStable(a, b) {
  const groupOrder = a.groupOrder - b.groupOrder;
  if (groupOrder !== 0) return groupOrder;
  const zOrder = a.sorting ? ... 
  // Actually the modern three.js uses:
  const objectId = a.id - b.id;
  if (zOrder !== 0) return zOrder;
  return objectId;
}
```
So it's groupOrder (renderOrder), then zOrder (depth), then objectId. So id is the final tie-break. Confirmed: the new stars (higher id after rebuild) would draw over the sun.

